# Aether engineering rules

Use these rules for Aether work, including integration outside `Aether/`.
Read `Docs/AETHER.md` before changing semantics. It is the versioned contract
for implemented behavior. Local plans and research notes are design references,
not an implicit extension of the implemented protocol. Keep the contract,
implementation, and meaningful tests aligned in the same change. Clearly mark
unsupported behavior; do not silently approximate it with another resolver.

## Replace the Replica sync path

`Aether/` stays until that path is gone. A rename waits. New work replaces
Replica sync and writes the existing scene objects. Do not grow a second scene
graph. The contribution store remains: it holds writer versions, not the
displayed object.

A field or entity that has moved has one path. The old deserialize path must
not write it. Projecting a resolved value is not a local edit and must not be
sent again. A Create, Update, or FullSync blob is not an operation. Field
versions, the original writer, and the rule version need a versioned wire
format. Do not invent them from arrival order.

An old delete must not drop a handled entity unless the reducer stores the
tombstone. A subscription may filter transport. It does not grant publication
membership or authority.

The label of an existing entity is on the wire. Reconnect of the same link
sends this peer's own current contribution for that label, and only after the
reducer applied it. Restart is separate: loading the reducer does not rebuild
the link. A new link sends the saved own records as a contribution merge.
The receiver queues that merge on the host. Commit journals it as state, not as an operation.
Check every (key, version) pair before choosing a winner.
The same version must carry the same value, operation id, and sequence.
A validated actor record moves that actor's frontier even when the field loses.
The same sequence repeats only the same operation id and Lamport.
A greater sequence needs a greater Lamport.
An inconsistent claim is rejected before the clock, the records, or the frontier change.
An admitted operation records that same frontier. The check happens before the write.
A missing actor id or a sequence below 1 is rejected before any change.
A sequence-only restore does not replace a recorded frontier.
A remote write or contribution is admitted only when its writer and its actor peer
are the principal bound to the delivering connection. The actor text is length-prefixed
principal and session. A prefix match is not enough. Relay is refused. The check
happens before the queue. The socket slot only routes. The process instance id can
change on every start. The durable principal is a 256-bit id in an explicit profile
directory. One process holds that profile. One profile is the whole configuration
directory. The file has a magic, a version, and a checksum. A damaged file does
not open. A version 1 file is not opened. Its random id is not this key's
fingerprint, so a new profile is required. The key file is readable only by
its owner, and the profile directory is private before the key is written.
The temporary key is flushed, then renamed, and the profile directory is
flushed so the new name survives power loss. A recovered temporary file is
opened exclusively, checked as a canonical P-256 key, flushed again, and only
then renamed. An existing principal is accepted only after that directory is
flushed again. The parent of the profile directory must already exist.
Open may create the profile directory, and it flushes that parent before
it accepts the principal.
The checksum shows integrity, not secrecy.
A handshake public key must be the canonical P-256 encoding, with no extra bytes.
The principal id is the fingerprint of the profile's public key.
`IsAuthenticated` is true only after both sides sign a fresh transcript.
A remote state packet is admitted only from an authenticated peer.
An unauthenticated claim is a development switch and stays off unless requested.
A failed handshake does not enter the queue. A replay fails because the
transcript includes this connection's fresh nonce. A send the transport does
not accept stays pending as the same bytes. The proof authenticates a principal's
initial self-create: writer, actor peer, creator, and initial owner must all be that
principal before the request enters the queue. Authenticated Create, Transfer, and
Delete carry the original principal public key and an end-to-end signature over a
versioned canonical envelope. Verify the fingerprint, signature, operation id, entity,
effect, version, and lifecycle metadata before the host queue. Preserve that proof in
the wire, snapshot, log, and catch-up envelope. A signature proves authorship; reducer
rules grant authority. Delete requires the authenticated principal to equal the current owner. Ownership transfer is explicit, is issued by
that current owner, and names the operation that established it. Keep creator,
initial owner, and current owner separate. Never resolve two transfers from the same
prior ownership operation by arrival order or actor id; that is owner equivocation,
outside the valid input set. An exact replay of the current transfer remains idempotent after digest eviction. A missing predecessor may wait only in a bounded queue.
A loaded record does not need a live connection.
A local write is rejected before it is applied when its fields would not fit in
one contribution packet, or when its one label would not fit in one state packet.
The encoded size is counted before the payload is copied.
A state packet and a contribution count are rejected before a field array is built
when they pass the protocol maximum. Packets waiting for a principal are capped
by count, by bytes, and by time.
An unchanged merge is not appended.
The actor and the sequence are on the record, not parsed from the operation id.
An operation is resent only from an envelope this process still holds.
Do not rebuild a multi-field operation from one stored field.
Do not relay another writer's
contribution. Create uses its original operation envelope and an immutable stored
record; do not rebuild it from a field contribution. Do not fold creation, deletion,
or owner into the contribution packet. A full
queue is a visible refusal, not a silent send. Catch-up advances only while the
connection works, a pump runs, and a slot frees. A packet dropped because the
held queue is full needs another send. Projecting the resolved label is not a
local edit.
An outgoing label stays queued until the transport accepts it. Stopping the
link unsubscribes it from the network.

File transfer and live audio or video stay beside state sync. Keep full,
partial, and resumable downloads, and live byte streams. Aether records the
media reference, access, and durable playback state. The file service carries
the bytes. The stream path carries live frames. Do not put those payloads in
the reducer, the dedup window, or the scene log. Store an identifier, a hash,
and the metadata the field needs. Give file copy, scene updates, and live
audio separate limits. A file retries missing pieces. Live media drops samples
that are already too old.

## State sync is not a command

Guarantee and fault model version 1 in `Docs/AETHER.md` is the label
state-sync contract. Do not treat it as a sensor, machine-control, or
critical-configuration contract, and do not generalize the label wire into
those until that model names the fault, the guarantee, and the refusal.
A greater actor id is not a safety decision. Transport handoff is not
execution. A Lamport value is not freshness. An applied write is not durable
and not remotely applied.

## Keep the scope small

Implement the current slice. Do not add speculative infrastructure, a generic
database framework, a full operation history, or one graph per entity.
Reuse Automerge's engineering ideas where useful; do not assume its document,
counter, deletion, or sync semantics match Aether's contracts.

## Preserve determinism

- The same valid input state, policy, and membership facts must yield the same
  resolved view. Different read sets may legitimately yield different views.
- Keep operation identity, transport sequence, field version, causal context,
  and local clock separate. A Lamport value is not wall-clock freshness or proof
  that every smaller operation has arrived.
- Neither arrival order, dictionary enumeration, local timeouts, socket state,
  nor local cache eviction may decide durable shared-state semantics.
- Specify canonical encodings and identifier comparisons. Validate accepted
  identifier encodings so distinct accepted IDs cannot collapse during encoding.
- Distinguish valid-operation convergence from malformed or equivocating actors.
  Do not claim that a bounded digest cache detects all historical ID reuse.

## Bound memory and frame work

- Store current versioned state, not every update. A sum replaces one writer's
  raw contribution; it does not accumulate retransmitted operations.
- Give queues and caches explicit limits. Entry counts alone do not bound bytes
  when identifiers or values have unbounded size. Define overflow behavior.
- Cache eviction is not protocol closure. Forgetting an ID must not discard an
  unseen valid write. Replay requires retained payload, not just a digest.
- Tombstones, membership, session metadata, and pending operations need explicit
  retention/recovery rules too. Do not describe an ever-growing auxiliary map
  as bounded merely because field-record count is stable.
- Prefer work proportional to changed state. Avoid scanning and sorting the
  entire store for every entity each frame. Measure before adding optimizations.
- Ephemeral poses/cursors use latest-sample buffers outside the durable store.
  Durable final state uses the ordinary validated reducer path.

## Validate before mutation

- Operations own immutable payloads. Digests use unambiguous, type-tagged,
  canonical encoding and a fixed-size hash.
- Validate operation kind, field shape, schema, version conflicts, and numeric
  limits before changing records, clocks, dedup state, or lifecycle metadata.
- For snapshot merge, validate all relevant key/version pairs, including lower
  versions hidden by a newer row. Apply the same validated folded representation.
- Expected protocol rejection leaves semantic state, clock, and cache unchanged.
  Define batch failure behavior separately; a frame is not implicitly a transaction.
- Guard arithmetic before mutation. Sum raw fixed-point contributions with a
  sufficiently wide accumulator; clamp only the resolved total. Preserve raw values.

## Keep meanings separate

- Creator, owner, original writer, actor session, and carrier are different roles.
  Preserve their meaning when adding authentication, relay, or recovery.
- Create records the registered type, creator, initial owner, version, operation id,
  and actor sequence. The creator is who issued Create, not who first appeared in a
  local scene. A conflicting natural-key Create is rejected before mutation. An
  Update may wait as raw state for Create; it never becomes Create.
- Delete is terminal for an entity ID. Hide changes shared visibility. Withdraw
  changes one publication membership. Field writes and heartbeat do not restore them.
- Keep the authenticated Delete claim with the tombstone so restart catch-up can
  send its canonical envelope after Create. A Delete received before Create waits
  in a bounded dependency queue. It never supplies creation or ownership facts.
- Compose the actual shared projection from deletion, visibility, and relevant
  publication/read membership. Storing a membership flag is insufficient if the
  frame or API projection ignores it. Preserve raw data as the contract requires.
- Paths and grouped natural-key views do not silently redefine identity or rights.
- An owner-only parent field does not prove a cycle-free tree. Tree repair must
  be deterministic for the same candidate-edge set and preserve raw candidates.

## Use ComponentGraph as the frame boundary

Compile a small, fixed `ComponentGraph` into `FrozenPlan` before ticking.
Stages call the same reducer exercised by direct tests. Graph topology alone
does not prove convergence. Keep local and remote durable operations on the
same admission path. Only the designated COMMIT stage writes the scene.
Disk and socket work must not block the tick; completion arrives through a
bounded inbox. Pending lookup never turns an Update into a Create.

## Treat persistence as protocol state

A saved image contains raw state and the metadata needed to continue safely,
not only rendered values. An old or partial image must not resurrect deletion
or withdrawn membership; absence alone is not deletion.
Local save is not policy cutover. Restore and merge are distinct operations.
Document clock/session recovery, snapshot scope, and durable acknowledgement.
When persistence is implemented, test interrupted snapshot replacement, journal
replay, and truncated tails. Do not require full historical replay for startup.

Bootstrap checkpoints are bounded, versioned, canonical state heads, not a hidden
history. Scope authority per entity. Verify an out-of-band pin before parsing state,
then verify the current owner's signature and retained lifecycle proofs before
installing a fresh reducer. An embedded signing key is not its own trust root.
Keep checkpoint I/O and chunk transport outside the frame. Do not call a bootstrap
checkpoint a policy cutover or dedup-coverage proof unless it explicitly carries
and validates those contracts.

Move checkpoint bytes through the generic content-addressed file path. Manifests
must bound total bytes, chunk bytes, and chunk count before allocation. Treat the
externally supplied whole-artifact digest as the trust input; a manifest checksum
only detects damage. Verify exact chunk length and hash before storage, verify stored
chunks again on resume and assembly, and verify the complete pinned artifact before
installing it. Resume from verified chunks rather than retaining a full transfer in
RAM. Give requests and chunk sends a fair budget beside state updates and live media.

## Test contracts, not just implementation agreement

Use handwritten expected results and adversarial small examples. Cover relevant
arrival permutations, duplicates, cache eviction, delayed lower versions,
snapshot/live interleaving, rejection without mutation, and numeric boundaries.
Test the composed frame projection as well as raw reducer state. Direct and
FrozenPlan agreement is useful but does not replace independent expectations.
Add each discovered counterexample as a regression. Verify bounded memory
across many distinct operations, not only stable contribution-record count.
Follow the repository's test-running rule. Report what actually ran and what
remains unimplemented; green tests do not establish untested protocol guarantees.
